WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.400
If you own an auto repair shop, a collision center, a detailing business, a glass company, or a tire shop — think about the last 30 days.

00:06.400 --> 00:10.200
How many estimates went unanswered?

00:10.200 --> 00:14.600
And how many of those ad dollars actually became profitable work?

00:14.600 --> 00:18.400
Now ask yourself: who is watching all of that?

00:18.400 --> 00:25.200
You already have the software. CRM, shop management, Google Ads, Jobber, CallRail, QuickBooks — and maybe a dozen other tools.

00:25.200 --> 00:28.800
None of them are asking what you should do next.

00:28.800 --> 00:34.400
That's still somebody's job. Follow up the $1,800. Catch the campaign. Rebuild the numbers. Your office manager — or you.

00:34.400 --> 00:39.800
Google Ads can be watched continuously while spend goes up and leads go down.

00:39.800 --> 00:45.200
Your SEO can be watched for changes. "Brake repair near me" sliding from number four to number seven.

00:45.200 --> 00:52.000
Your CRM can watch every open estimate. James, $1,800, four days silent. Elena, brakes, no follow-up. Omar, tires, not contacted.

00:52.000 --> 00:57.200
A customer received an $1,800 estimate. Instead of discovering it next Tuesday, an agent can catch it the same day.

00:57.200 --> 01:04.000
It checks history, prepares the follow-up under your rules, writes it back to the CRM, and records what happened.

01:04.000 --> 01:09.200
When a job is completed, a review request can go out. Flagged work stays flagged until a person closes it.

01:09.200 --> 01:15.600
It doesn't stop with customers. Your management team can have AI watching revenue, average repair order, marketing, and retention.

01:15.600 --> 01:22.000
Every morning you open five dashboards. You should get the exceptions: what changed, what matters, and what needs you.

